#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7221697-c245-573c-be7d-652163f6e37f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254323} {:answer/id #uuid "8b882184-5e97-5056-9ec0-a1434002a13a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254324} {:answer/id #uuid "10d7958e-1868-5d93-8a9d-23272d7e1433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254325} {:answer/id #uuid "fa815d57-dd6d-544f-bee3-ecf188611a16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254326} {:answer/id #uuid "5b605b48-1f56-5b52-a0a4-da41b6e648f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7221697-c245-573c-be7d-652163f6e37f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254323} :answer-field/id #uuid "6be6948b-0bbe-50e4-8e11-a063db8462a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254322}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6369285e-2dff-5b5c-a6d1-64b6011c7055" :question/math-academy-id "q-112960" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=6{x}^{3}-x-2,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 2.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{3},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcedd9d0-f87c-58b2-9ef1-c9fa0ef2e5c1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Unknown Value Given the Inverse of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe3cf74c-c351-566a-8f09-ee626bc9dfa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189914} {:answer/id #uuid "25545051-3fad-5bfe-94f3-f353b157211d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189915} {:answer/id #uuid "32bf33d7-b71f-5141-9684-1568365fe303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189916} {:answer/id #uuid "38c3b7ec-14c1-5ba8-975f-c825e9cc94b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189917} {:answer/id #uuid "45488d51-0034-5630-a527-ce41b7ba02e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38c3b7ec-14c1-5ba8-975f-c825e9cc94b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189917} :answer-field/id #uuid "71267b43-73be-50b2-ba7b-d193dc1f3d98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189913}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d4ef38d-4b7a-5aa7-8c47-cb116b290971" :question/math-academy-id "q-148300" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$, defined as\n\n$f(x) = \\frac{x + k}{9 - x}, x \\ne 9$\nhas an inverse function given by\n$f^{-1}(x) = \\frac{9x + 7}{x + 1}, x \\ne - 1$.\nWhat is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x+k}{9-x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{y+k}{9-y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{y+k}{9-y} \\\\ x(9-y) & =y+k \\\\ 9x-xy & =y+k \\\\ 9x-k & =xy+y \\\\ 9x-k & =y(x+1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{9x-k}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9x-k}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3ee7d83-2897-53ea-aa8f-05d52cb1f9e7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "438a5944-ebfe-51ee-be3a-1cd5914046e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "438a5944-ebfe-51ee-be3a-1cd5914046e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232243} :answer-field/id #uuid "494d2889-dfa2-5dec-b235-3a66dd264b0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070977}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be6f8905-1467-503f-ba88-c62f8817fc60" :question/math-academy-id "q-262211" :question/problem "The distance between the points $A(3, 5,-1)$ and $B(4, 3, 1)$ is {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(3,5,-1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(4,3,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(4-3)^{2}+(3-5)^{2}+(1-(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c16a30bc-0c1a-54f2-95d6-47edb0c85736" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Product of the Roots of a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3de3be6d-b395-5d09-bbab-10dfdc21ffb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254330} {:answer/id #uuid "424f0306-7c2f-54b9-bf17-95e7ad25a8e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254331} {:answer/id #uuid "f6fde9e0-3b3e-5a8a-8414-2ccd2c3ac7c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254332} {:answer/id #uuid "57bba0ba-0886-5ff7-9dbe-f633d561ea32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254333} {:answer/id #uuid "7faa68bf-704a-519a-a436-11963299936c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6fde9e0-3b3e-5a8a-8414-2ccd2c3ac7c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254332} :answer-field/id #uuid "f53737ff-b1fd-54c6-926c-e2c069766dff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254329}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3e9b421-3e24-576c-915f-687f04eee71b" :question/math-academy-id "q-288816" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-17x-30=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=6,\\,b=-17,\\,c=-30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-30)}{6}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "833ac63c-235b-5059-ab7c-56eb1e44d79e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Second Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b5b2859-3640-5b81-8651-2225339b75bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b5b2859-3640-5b81-8651-2225339b75bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254337} :answer-field/id #uuid "078ceede-3c63-5d57-a57a-728736efe9bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254336}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54147b52-7d93-5805-b5d5-f24fa1587a93" :question/math-academy-id "q-319506" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the **exact value** of the argument of $z=-3+3\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947117/assets/d00bb449e3525bed0543e690ef41895da5ad9bd28632f127aa7d8e5876ce7deb.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1bddb74-97be-5f35-b559-afb707e6e964" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Root Rule to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b5cfed7-5fb5-5f34-8a2a-2258069e7061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b5cfed7-5fb5-5f34-8a2a-2258069e7061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254340} :answer-field/id #uuid "75721307-a0a5-5c60-97d9-f2a1a5a6ed29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254339}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dc70fedc-2c3c-5334-9413-78a7c2f33957" :question/math-academy-id "q-338404" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the limit.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x+5}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x+5}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(3x+5)}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x)}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator separately.\n\nFor the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}(3x+5)=3(1)+5=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the root rule (continuity) in the denominator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x) & =\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{2}+2(1)+5}+2(1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{1+2+5}+2 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8}+2 \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x+5}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d4a21cd-bd8d-5572-b932-76875827170a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Fibonacci Sequence Given in Sequence Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b57ededd-87ea-5ae8-bb62-ba2c70c0d7a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053311} {:answer/id #uuid "cf480c0c-6505-5d0e-ab1b-aa96690bd876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053312} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf14017-6588-5643-8d32-b4803bcc9b2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053313} {:answer/id #uuid "280c51c1-39bf-5cff-87b4-e77720e22d02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053314} {:answer/id #uuid "632d39a6-880f-54ef-81cd-68bbcf4ce7ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf480c0c-6505-5d0e-ab1b-aa96690bd876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053312} :answer-field/id #uuid "bde78249-3237-5bee-911a-27bbc35d2924" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053310}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19a912bf-ebee-520e-9f08-2d308be0b8e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-51841" :question/problem "If $a_{n + 2} = a_{n + 1} + a_{n}$ with $a_{1} = 0$ and $a_{2} = 1$, then $a_{3} =$" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1+2} & ={a}_{1+1}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =1+0 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f149f8ef-dd7f-5f65-928e-534e1541f9b2" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Rational Expressions with a Single Common Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f3642e8-9632-58e1-b3d5-5a285bde9ee8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5b}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068094} {:answer/id #uuid "bafcbd8f-2ac6-5ee1-9e5c-e0fa4732ed87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7b}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068095} {:answer/id #uuid "d97cca86-3171-5afa-a41b-809e3967d762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35b^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068096} {:answer/id #uuid "0e6d988a-034d-5be3-bf95-327926b1137b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35b}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068097} {:answer/id #uuid "5d70e514-6681-5111-8c74-33427fc4d41b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e6d988a-034d-5be3-bf95-327926b1137b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35b}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068097} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9684ce8-4697-501f-aa63-afdcf6064c12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068093}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17f8926a-edda-5159-87e5-d53ce755ff9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-69924" :question/problem "What is $\\frac{5b^{2}}{2} \\div \\frac{b}{7}$?" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{b}^{2}}{2}÷\\frac{b}{7},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{b}^{2}}{2}÷\\frac{b}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{5{b}^{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{b} & = \\\\ \\frac{35{b}^{2}}{2b} & = \\\\ \\frac{35{b}^{\\cancel{2}}}{2\\cancel{b}} & = \\\\ \\frac{35b}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of $b$ in the numerator and denominator."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "078c9361-898f-5b9f-889e-c92442f882d9" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Constant Factor Rule to Integrate a Constant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b008cf83-77c5-5d7b-a5ef-10a4ec4a4d9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053995} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d6912f-411c-5737-af54-bf6f08dfe5b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053996} {:answer/id #uuid "29f12bf2-283d-54e3-865f-781433f438da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{y} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053997} {:answer/id #uuid "657dc64a-9662-59e8-bed6-f6a396dfca33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y^{2}} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053998} {:answer/id #uuid "6d6d23a1-3ea2-5d30-9d6a-670d65ef7584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2y + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d6d23a1-3ea2-5d30-9d6a-670d65ef7584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2y + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053999} :answer-field/id #uuid "a36802c4-882d-5ef8-9107-fb4cc29f6a9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053994}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91c31b0e-fbc7-5f72-b266-859c50fd9d6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-71142" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int(-2)dy$." :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-2)\\,\\text{d}y & =-2\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =-2\\cdot y+C \\\\  & =-2y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254341 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecd367ac-2a68-56d0-b553-85277a9c921d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Secant or Cosecant of a Special Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b90d25f3-4baf-58b7-ba76-959ff7f39d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254343} {:answer/id #uuid "174c89ad-c596-549c-be65-dea6cf4cbf97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254344} {:answer/id #uuid "cd996b13-7796-55ba-890e-b7acf2cf78fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254345} {:answer/id #uuid "b9031b94-926d-5f93-848a-4e524bbb0eb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254346} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e40331-f8f6-5b35-bb34-2cf41b5efe7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7e40331-f8f6-5b35-bb34-2cf41b5efe7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254347} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc925353-254d-59c1-84b4-1fc5165b70fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254342}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "284d9cbf-5f0f-5fe0-8b82-9cf1281e3bcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-86328" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947117/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})}=\\frac{1}{-\\frac{1}{2}}=-2.\n$$"}]}
